 date packages you don't need to run  calculations on the subserver  you can really scale a lot so and easily  so you don't have to maintain an  expensive and  like complex infrastructure  server infrastructure to serve all those  users you just have to serve static  files  so for instance uh currently uh biodiet  has around  50 to 80 000 downloads per month and  so potentially there is like a few  tens of thousands users uh potential new  users  each month and if we wanted to  serve those users uh to to make them use  um with the server-based uh  architecture that would be difficult so  for instance um  the binder project uh allows you to run  um  you know to run jupyter instances and  and scale scale them to a large number  of users and it's it's a fairly complex  and and  successful and challenging project so uh  of course you can do much more in but in  binder that what you would be able to do  with iodide because it's uh  it's more let's say less experimental  but  but the idea is that with iodide  essentially you can you can just  transfer static files and  that scales extremely well so what  should be also noted is that  packages are downloaded once and then  they are cached in the browser so this  is also good for privacy because  uh well you don't you don't make too  much remote requests and also  uh generally while calculations are run  locally so you don't need to send your  data or at least not as much data to the  remote server  a last point which is important is that  uh in the in the case of algorithms  or like some scientific code  well the python app in the browser would  be just some archive of  python files and uh maybe some python  objects  and so this you can you can easily  examine this on the client side so if  you if you go to a  website that uses such an app you can  just examine what's happening whether  it's a code used etc  and so this is uh this is good for  transparency but  in other use cases that can be also a  bit problematic if you 